  "scenes": {
    "sm-detail": {
      "anchor": "sm-array",
      "offset": [1.5, 3.5, 1.6],
      "components": [
        {
          "id": "smem",
          "name": "Combined L1 / Shared memory",
          "group": "memory",
          "color": "#e0a33a",
          "geom": {
            "type": "box",
            "size": [3.4, 0.2, 2.4],
            "pos": [0, 0, 0]
          },
          "desc": "Volta unifies L1 data cache and shared memory into one 128 KB block per SM, configurable (e.g. up to 96 KB shared / 32 KB L1). CUDA cores explicitly load A/B tiles here with LDG/STS — there is no TMA or async-copy engine yet (both arrive with Ampere's `cp.async`, then Hopper's TMA).",
          "specs": [
            {
              "label": "L1 + shared (per SM)",
              "value": "128 KB, configurable (up to 96 KB shared)",
              "source": "https://images.nvidia.com/content/volta-architecture/pdf/volta-architecture-whitepaper.pdf"
            }
          ],
          "role": "shared-memory"
        },
        {
          "id": "tensor-core",
          "parent": "smem",
          "name": "Tensor Core (1st gen)",
          "group": "compute",
          "color": "#2a58b8",
          "geom": {
            "type": "box",
            "size": [0.38, 0.35, 0.9],
            "pos": [-1.4525, 0.5, -0.55]
          },
          "repeat": {
            "count": [8, 1, 1],
            "step": [0.415, 0, 0]
          },
          "desc": "The first Tensor Core. Each clock, a warp issues `mma.sync` (HMMA): a 4×4×4 FP16 multiply with FP32 accumulate per core — one 4×4 warp-level matrix multiply-add per Tensor Core, 2 per processing block (8 per SM). Operands come straight from registers loaded out of shared memory; there is no FP8/FP4 support and no separate accumulator memory (Blackwell's TMEM is 3 generations away).",
          "specs": [
            {
              "label": "MMA shape / precision",
              "value": "4×4×4 FP16 multiply, FP32 accumulate",
              "source": "https://images.nvidia.com/content/volta-architecture/pdf/volta-architecture-whitepaper.pdf"
            },
            {
              "label": "Tensor Cores per SM",
              "value": "8 (2 per processing block × 4)",
              "source": "https://images.nvidia.com/content/volta-architecture/pdf/volta-architecture-whitepaper.pdf"
            }
          ],
          "role": "tensor-core"
        },
        {
          "id": "register-file",
          "parent": "smem",
          "name": "Register file",
          "group": "memory",
          "color": "#d07a40",
          "geom": {
            "type": "box",
            "size": [0.7, 0.25, 0.9],
            "pos": [-1.25, 0.45, 0.55]
          },
          "repeat": {
            "count": [4, 1, 1],
            "step": [0.83, 0, 0]
          },
          "desc": "Per-thread registers, one 64 KB bank per processing block (256 KB total per SM). mma.sync reads operands from and accumulates results into these registers — there is no dedicated Tensor Memory.",
          "specs": [
            {
              "label": "Size (per SM)",
              "value": "256 KB (4 × 64 KB)",
              "source": "https://images.nvidia.com/content/volta-architecture/pdf/volta-architecture-whitepaper.pdf"
            }
          ],
          "role": "register-file"
        },
        {
          "id": "warp-scheduler",
          "parent": "register-file",
          "name": "Warp scheduler + CUDA cores",
          "group": "control",
          "geom": {
            "type": "box",
            "size": [0.7, 0.15, 0.9],
            "pos": [-1.25, 0.75, 0.55]
          },
          "repeat": {
            "count": [4, 1, 1],
            "step": [0.83, 0, 0]
          },
          "desc": "Each of the 4 processing blocks has its own warp scheduler + dispatch unit, 16 FP32 cores, 16 INT32 cores and 8 FP64 cores (64/64/32 per SM). These run the epilogue — bias add, activation — in FP32/INT32, and handle double-precision (HPC) work.",
          "role": "warp-scheduler"
        }
      ]
    }
  },
  "flows": [
    {
      "id": "hbm-left",
      "from": "hbm-dram-left",
      "to": "die",
      "group": "memory",
      "particles": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "hbm-right",
      "from": "hbm-dram-right",
      "to": "die",
      "group": "memory",
      "particles": 2
    }
  ],
  "steps": [
    {
      "text": "1. Weights and activations sit in HBM2 (16/32 GB, 900 GB/s on SXM2/SXM3).",
      "focus": ["hbm-dram-left", "hbm-dram-right", "hbm-base-left", "hbm-base-right"],
      "dot": "hbm-dram-left",
      "explode": 0.6,
      "drill": null,
      "stage": "hbm"
    },
    {
      "text": "2. Memory controllers read the HBM2 stacks.",
      "focus": ["mc-left"],
      "dot": "mc-left",
      "stage": "memory-controller"
    },
    {
      "text": "3. Data lands in the 6 MB L2 cache.",
      "focus": ["l2"],
      "dot": "l2",
      "stage": "l2"
    },
    {
      "text": "4. CUDA cores issue explicit LDG/STS loads to copy A/B tiles into shared memory — no TMA or async-copy engine exists yet (Ampere adds `cp.async`; Hopper adds TMA).",
      "focus": ["smem"],
      "dot": "smem",
      "drill": "sm-detail",
      "stage": "shared-memory"
    },
    {
      "text": "5. Threads issue LDS loads, moving operand tiles from shared memory into registers.",
      "focus": ["register-file"],
      "dot": "register-file",
      "drill": "sm-detail",
      "stage": "load-operands"
    },
    {
      "text": "6. A warp issues `mma.sync` (HMMA): each of the 8 Tensor Cores per SM multiplies a 4×4×4 FP16 tile.",
      "focus": ["tensor-core"],
      "dot": "tensor-core",
      "drill": "sm-detail",
      "stage": "tensor-core"
    },
    {
      "text": "7. Partial products accumulate in FP32 registers — 1st-gen Tensor Cores have no separate accumulator memory (Blackwell's TMEM is 3 generations away).",
      "focus": ["register-file"],
      "dot": "register-file",
      "drill": "sm-detail",
      "stage": "accumulate"
    },
    {
      "text": "8. Epilogue: the processing block's FP32/INT32 cores add bias and apply activation.",
      "focus": ["warp-scheduler", "register-file"],
      "dot": "warp-scheduler",
      "drill": "sm-detail",
      "stage": "epilogue"
    },
    {
      "text": "9. Results write back to HBM2; NVLink 2 (300 GB/s) exchanges tiles with peer GPUs for multi-GPU training.",
      "focus": ["nvlink"],
      "dot": "nvlink",
      "drill": null,
      "stage": "writeback"
    }
  ],
